# Sustained-Release Nanocapsules Enable Long-Lasting Stabilization of Li Anode for Practical Li-Metal Batteries

## abstract
A robust solid-electrolyte interphase (SEI) enabled by electrolyte additive is a promising approach to stabilize Li anode and improve Li cycling efficiency. However, the self-sacrificial nature of SEI forming additives limits their capability to stabilize Li anode for long-term cycling. Herein, we demonstrate nanocapsules made from metal-organic frameworks for sustained release of LiNO3 as surface passivation additive in commercial carbonate-based electrolyte. The nanocapsules can offer over 10 times more LiNO3 than the solubility of LiNO3. Continuous supply of LiNO3 by nanocapsules forms a nitride-rich SEI layer on Li anode and persistently remedies SEI during prolonged cycling. As a result, lifespan of thin Li anode in 50 μm, which experiences drastic volume change and repeated SEI formation during cycling, has been notably improved. By pairing with an industry-level thick LiCoO2 cathode, practical Li-metal full cell demonstrates a remarkable capacity retention of 90% after 240 cycles, in contrast to fast capacity drop after 60 cycles in LiNO3 saturated electrolyte.

## HIGHLIGHTS
•	 Nanocapsules made from metal–organic frameworks were designed for sustained release of additive $\mathrm{(LiNO}_{3})$ ) to passivate Li anode in commercial carbonate-based electrolyte.   
• The nanocapsules with continuous supply of $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ formed a nitride-rich solid electrolyte interphase layer on Li anode and persistently remedied the interphase during prolonged cycling. The practical Li-metal full cell delivered a prolonged lifespan with $90\%$ capacity retention after 240 cycles which has been hardly achieved in commercial electrolyte.
ABSTRACT  A robust solid-electrolyte interphase (SEI) enabled by electrolyte additive is a promising approach to stabilize Li anode and improve Li cycling efficiency. However, the self-sacrificial nature of SEI forming additives limits their capability to stabilize Li anode for long-term cycling. Herein, we demonstrate nanocapsules made from metal–organic frameworks for sustained release of $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ as surface passivation additive in commercial carbonate-based electrolyte. The nanocapsules can offer over 10 times more $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ than the solubility of $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ . Continuous supply of $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ by nanocapsules forms a nitride-rich SEI layer on Li
anode and persistently remedies SEI during prolonged cycling. As a result, lifespan of thin Li anode in $50\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , which experiences drastic volume change and repeated SEI formation during cycling, has been notably improved. By pairing with an industry-level thick $\mathbf{LiCoO}_{2}$ cathode, practical Li-metal full cell demonstrates a remarkable capacity retention of $90\%$ after 240 cycles, in contrast to fast capacity drop after 60 cycles in $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ saturated electrolyte.
KEYWORDS  Metal–organic frameworks; $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ ; Nanocapsules; Lithium-metal anode; Lithium-metal batteries
## 1  Introduction
Li-metal batteries (LMBs) have attracted great attention in recent years due to the much improved energy density enabled by the use of Li-metal anode (LMA). However, Li dendrites growth and low Li cycling efficiency result in quick failure of the electrode and safety hazards [1–4]. Moreover, a prerequisite of high-energy-density LMBs is the successful adaptation of thin LMA with limited Li, which requires effective methods to improve the Columbic efficiency (CE) of LMA [3, 5, 6]. Solid-electrolyte interphase (SEI) layer is undoubtedly the key for durable LMA. A mechanically and chemically robust SEI layer with high ionic conductivity promotes homogeneous ion flux and uniform Li deposition/dissolution, thus minimizing dendrites formation and improving the CE of LMA [7–11]. Incorporating additives in electrolyte that are preferentially reduced on LMA (e.g., fluoroethylene carbonate (FEC) [12], lithium difluoro(oxalato)borate (LiDFOB) [13], and $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ [14]) represents a feasible and efficient approach to manipulate the SEI [15–17].
In particular, $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ has been demonstrated as an effective additive or co-salt in ether-based electrolytes for Li–S and $\mathrm{Li}{-}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ batteries, forming stable SEI on LMA enriched with inorganic compounds of $\mathrm{LiN}_{x}\mathrm{O}_{y}$ and $\mathrm{Li}_{3}\mathrm{N}$ [18–20]. Such highly ionic conductive SEI facilitates ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ transport and regulates the deposited Li into granular structure, which enhances Li cycling efficiency and prohibits dendriteinduced short circuits. Nevertheless, adaptation of $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ in carbonate-based electrolytes, which are mature and compatible with high-voltage cathodes (e.g., $\mathbf{LiCoO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{LiNi}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{y}\mathrm{Mn}_{z}\mathrm{O}_{2})$ , has been limited by its extremely low solubility in carbonate solvents (about $0.8~\mathrm{{mg}~\mathrm{{mL^{-1}}}}$ ) [21]. $\mathrm{CuF}_{2}$ has been found to increase the solubility of $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ to $1.0\mathrm{wt}\%$ $\mathrm{\Omega}^{}10\mathrm{{mg}\ m L^{-1}},$ ) in carbonate electrolyte by forming $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}{-\mathrm{NO}_{3}}^{-}$ complex [22], yet the possible reduction of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ might compromise the stability of this system. Alternatively, solid $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ has been incorporated in LMBs by preimpregnating on porous separator [23], intercalating between bilayer separators [24], or encapsulating in polymer gel [21, 25] to overcome the solubility limit of $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ , however, at the cost of extra weight gain and possibly blocked ionic transport. To this end, efficient method to continuously stabilize LMAs with high-ionic conductivity nitride-rich SEI in carbonatebased electrolyte for practical LMBs has not been achieved.
Inspired by drug delivery systems for disease treatment [26, 27], we demonstrate sustained-release nanocapsules made from nanoparticles of metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) to continuously stabilize LMA with $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ additive in carbonate-based electrolyte. While MOFs have been used to immobilize ionic species as electrolytes for battery applications [28–31], there is no attempt to manipulate the SEI-forming components in commercial electrolytes with the assistance of MOFs. In this study, MOF nanoparticles can uptake a substantial amount of $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ (denoted as $\operatorname{LNO}@$ MOF) and well disperse in liquid electrolyte. More than ten times of the solubility limit of $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ can be introduced into electrolyte. During battery operation, $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ is continuously consumed to remedy the SEI layer and replenished by the nanocapsules. Here we show that thin LMA of $50~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ can be effectively stabilized in commercial carbonate-based electrolyte containing $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{LNO@MOF}}$ nanocapsules. When coupled with a thick $\mathbf{LiCoO}_{2}$ cathode $(21\mathrm{\mg\cm}^{-2})$ , the as-assembled practical LMB full cell demonstrates outstanding cycle stability with a capacity retention of $90\%$ after 240 cycles.
## 2  Experimental Section
## 2.1  Synthesis of MOF‑808 Nanoparticles
MOF-808 nanoparticles were prepared according to a previous report [32]. Briefly, $\mathrm{ZrOCl}_{2}{\cdot}8\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ( $0.97\mathrm{mg}$ , 3.0 mmol) and 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylate $\mathrm{(H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC)}$ $(0.21~\mathrm{{\mg}}$ , $1\mathrm{mmol}\AA,$ ) were dissolved in $N{,}N{\ }$ -dimethylformamide (DMF)/ formic acid $(30~\mathrm{mL}/30~\mathrm{mL})$ ) and loaded into a $100\mathrm{-mL}$ Teflon-lined autoclave and heated at $130^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for two days. After cooling to room temperature, the MOF-808 power was collected by filtration and washed by DMF for three times. Afterward, the sample was immersed in methanol for solvent exchange for 3 days, during each time methanol was replaced three times per day. Finally, MOF-808 was vacuumdried at room temperature and then at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10\mathrm{~h~}$ to yield MOF-808 nanoparticles.
## 2.2  Preparation of LNO@MOF Nanocapsules
The MOF-808 nanoparticles were immersed into $2{\bmod{\mathrm{L}}}^{-1}$ $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ methanol solution and stirred for $24\mathrm{h}$ to enable full impregnation of $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ into the pores of MOF-808. Then, the composites were washed by methanol for three times to remove residual $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ outside MOF-808 particles. Finally, $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{LNO@MOF}}$ was vacuum-dried at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10\mathrm{{h}}$ to remove methanol solvent.
## 2.3  Preparation of LNO $@$ MOF Electrolyte
In an argon-filled glovebox, $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{LNO@MOF}}$ nanocapsules were added into blank electrolyte in which $1\mathrm{MLiPF}_{6}$ was dissolved in ethylene carbonate (EC)/diethyl carbonate (DEC) ( $1/1$ , by volume) to reach certain concentrations (25, 50 and $100\mathrm{{mg}\ m L^{-1}}.$ ). The obtained electrolyte was stirred for $2\mathfrak{h}$ to ensure uniform dispersion. LNO $@$ MOF electrolyte containing $50\mathrm{{mg}\mathrm{{mL}^{-1}}}$ of L ${\mathrm{.NO}}@\mathbf{MOF}$ is used unless specified.
## 2.4  Materials Characterizations
was used as reference sample for $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{LNO@MOF}}$ electrolyte and blank electrolyte for $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ saturated electrolyte. The data were measured in the range of $300{\mathrm{-}}190~\mathrm{nm}$ . Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images and X-ray energy-dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) were performed using Phenom LE. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analysis was performed on Thermo Fisher Scientific Escalab 250Xi. For SEM and XPS analyses, the electrode disassembled in the argon filled glovebox was washed by dimethyl carbonate (DMC) for three time and vacuum-dried. A sealed container was used before transferring for further postmortem analysis. Electrolyte ionic conductivity was measured by electrochemical impedance spectroscopy with a two-electrode stainless steel cell with the cell constant of $0.637\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Viscosity of electrolytes was measured using HAAKE Rotational Rheometer (RS6000) with the parallel plate at a shear rate of $100\mathrm{{s}^{-1}}$ at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
## 2.5  Electrochemical Measurements
Li|Cu, Li|Li and $\mathrm{LilLiCoO}_{2}$ cells were prepared using CR2032 coin cells with $40~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ electrolyte in each cell and polypropylene membranes as the separator. The coin cells were monitored by a battery testing system (Neware, CT$4008{\cdot}5\mathrm{V}10\ \mathrm{mA};$ at $27\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The average Coulombic efficiency of Li|Cu coin cell was calculated based on a method developed by Zhang et al. [33]. Specifically, $5\ \mathrm{mAh\cm}^{-2}$ of Li was first plated on the $\mathtt{C u}$ substrate and stripped to $_{1\mathrm{~V~}}$ before depositing the Li reservoir $(Q_{\mathrm{T}}{=}5\ \mathrm{mAh\cm^{-2}})$ at $0.5\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , then cycling $(Q_{\mathrm{C}}{=}0.5\ \mathrm{mAh\cm^{-2}})$ for 20 cycles $(n=20)$ ), and finally dissolving all the remaining Li $(Q_{\mathrm{s}})$ to $_{1\mathrm{~V~}}$ . The average CE over n cycles is calculated as follows:
$$
\mathrm{CE}_{\mathrm{ave}}=\:\big(Q_{\mathrm{S}}+{{n}\o{Q_{\mathrm{C}}}}\big)/\big(Q_{\mathrm{T}}+{{n}\o{Q_{\mathrm{C}}}}\big)
$$
Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) test was measured on a Bio-Logic SAS (MPG2) with a frequency range of $20\mathrm{kHz}\mathrm{-}10\mathrm{mHz}$ and an applied voltage of $10\mathrm{mV}$ at $27^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . $\mathbf{LiCoO}_{2}$ cathode was prepared by mixing active material, super P and poly(vinylidene fluoride) (PVDF) with weight ratios of 96:2:2 in $N$ -methyl-2-pyrrolidone (NMP) and blade coated on Al foil and vacuum-dried at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Areal loading of $\mathbf{LiCoO}_{2}$ cathode was around $21\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . The cycled LCO|Li full cell was dissembled, and the Li anode was reassembled with stainless steel (SS) in blank electrolyte to measure the remaining capacity of the cycled lithium anode. The Li|SS cell was charged to $1.0\mathrm{V}$ at a current density of $0.2\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ .
## 3  Results and Discussion
## 3.1  Synthesis and Characterizations of LNO $@$ MOF Nanocapsules
Schematic illustration of the synthesis and working principle of the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{LNO@MOF}}$ nanocapsules is shown in Fig. 1a. MOF-808 was chosen as the host of nanocapsules due to its high surface area, large pore volume, high (electro-)chemical stability in organic electrolyte, easy synthesis and relatively low cost. The large adamantane cage in MOF-808 with an internal diameter of $18.4\mathring\mathrm{\AA}$ and a pore window of $14\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ enables efficient encapsulation and diffusion of $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ [34]. The
LNO $@$ MOF nanocapsules were prepared by impregnating MOF-808 with $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ methanol solution, followed by centrifugation and vacuum drying. The as-synthesized $\operatorname{LNO}@$ MOF nanocapsules can be easily dispersed in carbonatebased electrolyte, which release $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ into electrolyte until saturation. Consumption of $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ to form SEI on fresh Li would be rapidly compensated by the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{LNO@MOF}}$ nanocapsules, which keep the electrolyte in a $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ -saturated quasi-equilibrium state.
The as-prepared $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{LNO@MOF}}$ was characterized by XRD as shown in Fig. 1b. The XRD pattern of the $\operatorname{LNO}@$ MOF is closely matched with that of MOF-808, confirming that the crystal structure of MOF-808 remains intact after loading $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ . The absence of diffraction peaks from $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ confirms its encapsulation in the micropores of MOF-808 without long-range order. As shown by the $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms of MOF-808 (Figs.  1c and S1), the specific surface area and pore volume of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{LNO}}@{\mathrm{MOF}}$ are substantially reduced from $765.4\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $0.638\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (MOF-808) to $19.6\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $0.02{\mathrm{~cm}}^{3}{\mathrm{~g}}^{-1}$ , respectively, after incorporation of $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ . The reduced porosity verifies the hypothesis that $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ is mainly encapsulated into the pores of MOF808 rather than precipitates alongside the MOF particles. Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{LNO@MOF}}$ further verifies the successful incorporation of $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ as shown in Fig. 1d. A much higher weight loss of about $50\mathrm{\wt\%}$ between 400 and $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is observed for $\operatorname{LNO}@$ MOF compared to $28~\mathrm{wt}\%$ for pristine MOF-808, which could be attributed to the decomposition of $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ into $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) shows similar morphology of MOF-808 and $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{LNO}}@{\mathrm{MOF}}$ consisting of octahedral particles of $\sim500\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. S2). Uniform distribution of N and $Z\mathrm{r}$ elements in $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{LNO@MOF}}$ is revealed by X-ray energy-dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) elemental mapping (Fig. 1e). The loading amount of $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ in $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{LNO}}@{\mathrm{MOF}}$ is estimated at ${\sim}21~\mathrm{wt}\%$ based on inductively coupled plasma-optical emission spectrometry (ICP-OES) and EDS analysis (Table S1).
$\scriptstyle{\mathrm{LNO@MOF}}$ nanocapsules can be easily dispersed in commercial carbonate-based electrolyte ( $\mathrm{~1~M~LiPF_{6}}$ in ethylene carbonate (EC)/diethyl carbonate (DEC) of $1/1$ by volume) with a concentration of $50~\mathrm{{mg}~\mathrm{{mL^{-1}}}}$ to form a colloidal electrolyte (denoted as $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{LNO}}@{\mathrm{MOF}}$ electrolyte, Fig. S3a), corresponding to a ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}{}^{-}$ concentration of $11\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}(0.16\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{L}^{-1})$ . $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{LNO@MOF}}$ electrolyte exhibits slightly increased viscosity and reduced ionic conductivity compared with pristine electrolyte (Table S2), and maintains good wettability with separators (Fig. S3b). The sustained release of $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ from nanocapsules into electrolyte was monitored by ultraviolet light absorption (Fig. 1f) according to the strong absorption peak of ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}{}^{-}$ ion at around $205~\mathrm{nm}$ [35, 36]. Surprisingly, the absorbance of ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}{}^{-}$ in $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{LNO@MOF}}$ electrolyte is notably higher than that of $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ saturated electrolyte, implying a supersaturated ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}{}^{-}$ solution. A possible explanation is the absorption of $\mathrm{PF}_{6}^{-}$ by MOF-808 [28], which reduces the $\mathrm{PF}_{6}^{-}$ concentration and in turn increases ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}{}^{-}$ solubility [21]. After refreshing electrolyte, $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ concentration remains a high value until the third recycled electrolyte, revealing that nanocapsules help to maintain the $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ -saturated state of electrolyte, ensuring its long-term effect as a consumed additive during cycling.
## 3.2  Electrochemical Performance of LMA in LNO $@$ MOF Electrolyte
The interfacial stability of LMA in LNO $@$ MOF electrolyte is studied by Li|Li symmetric cells. As shown in Fig. 2a, the cycling stability of the cell with $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ saturated electrolyte is improved compared with that of blank electrolyte, however still fails after $530\mathrm{h}$ . Thus, $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ indeed enables a more stable interphase on LMA, yet the interphase would fail upon prolonged cycling. When using LNO $@$ MOF electrolyte, the cell can be cycled for more than $1000\mathrm{~h~}$ with a low overpotential below $100~\mathrm{{mV}}$ and no sign of short circuit. The extended lifespan of the LMA in $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{LNO}}@{\mathrm{MOF}}$ electrolyte could be attributed to the continuous release of $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ , which immediately repairs the damaged SEI during cycling. The charge transport characteristic of interfacial layer formed on lithium anode is studied by EIS as shown in Figs. 2b and S3. The interfacial resistance $(R_{\mathrm{int}})$ represented by the semicircle at high-frequency region in Nyquist plots decreases during cycling for the Li|Li cell using LNO $@$ MOF electrolyte, suggesting that the SEI is gradually stabilized and enriched with high ion-conductive species. This notably differs from the $R_{\mathrm{int}}$ of the cell with blank electrolyte, which decreases after the first 50 cycles and then increases afterward due to unstable SEI formation. The reaction kinetics of lithium plating/stripping was further examined by cyclic voltammetry (CV) tests of Cu|Li cells. As shown in Fig. 2c, the current response of initial Li plating/stripping is significantly enhanced in $\mathsf{L N O@M O F}$ electrolyte, corresponding to lower ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ transfer barriers through SEI and fast reaction kinetics. This signifies the contribution of interface layer formed by $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ decomposition that improves electrochemical kinetics of lithium deposition/dissolution.
Asymmetric cells were assembled using limited Li $(50\upmu\mathrm{m}$ Li corresponds to about $10\ \mathrm{mAh\cm^{-2}},$ ) as the working electrode to evaluated the durability of practical LMA. The cutoff voltage of $1.0\mathrm{V}$ was set as the complete consumption of active Li in the $50~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ Li foil. Figure 2d shows that the cell using $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{LNO}}@{\mathrm{MOF}}$ electrolyte exhibits an extended cycle life of $250\mathrm{h}$ , which is much longer than that of $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ saturated electrolyte $(150\mathrm{h})$ and blank electrolyte (112 h). Note that the concentration of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{LNO@MOF}}$ would be directly related to the maximal amount of released $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ , and a lower LNO $@$ MOF concentration of $25\mathrm{{mg}\mathrm{{mL^{-1}}}}$ leads to inferior cycling performance of the Li|Li cell. However, further increasing the concentration of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{LNO@MOF}}$ to
$100\ \mathrm{mg\mL^{-1}}$ leads to increased cell polarization (Fig. S5a), possibly due to the aggregation of nanocapsules that impedes the ionic transport. Our previous studies indicate that MOFs with abundant open-metal sites (OMSs) to immobilize anions would increase the ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ ion transference number of liquid electrolyte and improve the stability of LMA [37, 38]. However, due to the few OMSs in lowtemperature-treated MOF-808 and its relative low content, pristine MOF-808 in electrolyte only marginally improves the cycle life of LMA (Fig. S5b). The improvement of LMA in LNO $@$ MOF electrolyte would be mainly attributed to the released $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ from nanocapsules.
The average Coulombic efficiency (CE) of Li stripping/plating was determined by Cu|Li cells. Figure 2e shows that the average CE of the cell with $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{LNO}}@{\mathrm{MOF}}$ is $98.8\%$ , which is much higher than that of blank electrolyte $(82.4\%)$ . The voltage polarization is also reduced in $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{LNO}}@{\mathrm{MOF}}$ electrolyte, which is in line with the observation in EIS measurement. The CE of Cu|Li cells during cycling in Fig. S6 also shows that the high CE with $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{LNO}}@{\mathrm{MOF}}$ electrolyte could be maintained for 100 cycles without obvious decay. The results confirm that the sustained release of $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ from nanocapsules guarantees high lithium cycling efficiency with small voltage hysteresis.
## 3.3  Li Deposition Morphologies and Characterization of SEI
The morphology of Li deposited on limited Li ${\left\langle{50\ \upmu\mathrm{m}}\right\rangle}$ during cycling was examined by SEM. Figure 3a, b shows the morphology evolution of deposited Li with increased capacity at $1\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . With blank electrolyte (Fig. 3a), Li grows into dendritic structures along longitudinal with increased capacity, which easily cause excessive SEI formation, isolated Li and short circuit. In contrast, with the LNO@MOF electrolyte (Fig. 3b), Li prefers to grow laterally into granular structures with good uniformity instead of dendritic or whisker growth. As the cycle number increases, the morphology of the deposited Li could be generally maintained even after 100 cycles as show in Fig. 3c–e. In contrast, the deposited Li presents a porous and loose structure with random inactive Li dendrites during cycling in blank electrolyte (Fig. S7). The cross section views (inset of Fig. 3c–e) present that the top layer containing deposited Li and passivation layer is relatively dense and smooth. The thickness increases slowly during cycling, indicating less severe Li pulverization during repeated plating and stripping. The intact active Li on the Li foil remains about $60\%$ of the initial capacity of pristine Li foil after 100 cycles.
The morphology of deposited Li during cycling signifies the $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ -induced stable SEI in reducing Li dendrite growth and “dead Li” for practical LMA.
XPS analysis and EDS were carried out to study the chemical composition of SEI layer on Li surface (Figs. S8 and S9), consisting of various inorganic and organic moieties. N 1s XPS spectrum in Fig. 3f demonstrates that the main decomposition products of $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ on Li are $\mathrm{Li}_{3}\mathrm{N},\mathrm{LiN}_{x}\mathrm{O}_{y}$ and $\mathrm{LiNO}_{2}$ . Moreover, SEI is enriched with various inorganic Li compounds, especially with abundant N-containing species as revealed by Li 1s XPS spectrum in Fig. 3g. This inorganic-rich SEI facilitates the de-solvation process and ion diffusion through SEI, thus ensuring a stable and efficient lithium plating/stripping during cycling [39]. In addition, such nitride-enriched SEI is rather dense to prevent the decomposition of $\mathrm{LiPF}_{6}$ even after prolonged cycling, as revealed by the XPS spectra and EDS (Figs. S8 and S9).
## 3.4  Electrochemical Performance of Practical LMB
To demonstrate the implementation of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{LNO@MOF}}$ electrolyte in high-energy LMBs, we fabricate full cells using $\mathbf{LiCoO}_{2}$ (LCO) as cathode and thin LMA. Notably, a low negative-to-positive-capacity (N/P) ratio is imperative for high-energy density LMB [3, 6]. Hence, a commercial-level LCO cathode of around $3.0\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , a limited Li ${\langle}50{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ , $10\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{cm}^{-2},$ ) with a N/P ratio of around 3.3 and lean electrolyte of $13\upmu\mathrm{L}\:\mathrm{mAh}^{-1}$ were employed. Figure 4a shows that LCO|Li full cells with blank and $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ saturated electrolyte could operate stably for 21 and 62 cycles, respectively, followed by rapid decline in capacity and CE that is likely due to the fast consumption of the limited Li [7]. Notably, the full cell with $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{LNO@MOF}}$ electrolyte demonstrates an extraordinary cycle stability with a capacity retention of $90\%$ after 240 cycles. Voltage profiles (Fig. S10) also present low polarization for the cell with $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{LNO@MOF}}$ electrolyte in comparison with control cells with blank and $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ saturated electrolytes.
To quantify the exact Li loss during cycling, cycled Li anode was reassembled against a stainless steel (SS) electrode to measure the remaining active Li. Figure 4b shows $8.5\mathrm{mAh}$ of Li remains on the cycled LMA in $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ saturated electrolyte, which is consistent with original Li from the extra area of anode compared to cathode $\left(0.87\thinspace\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\right.$ corresponds to $8.7\mathrm{mAh}$ ). Therefore, there is actually no active
Li remains in the cycled area. In contrast, $14.9\ \mathrm{mAh}$ of Li remains in LNO $@$ MOF electrolyte, which corresponds to $5.5\ \mathrm{mAh\cm}^{-2}$ ( $\sim55\%$ of the initial capacity) of active Li remains in the cycled area. Optical image of the cycled LMA (inset in Fig. 4b) confirms the less corroded appearance in $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{LNO@MOF}}$ electrolyte compared with pulverized black powders detached from current collector in $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ saturated electrolyte. The SEM morphology of cycled lithium in Fig. S11 shows similar results. For LNO@MOF electrolyte, the cycled Li surface is smoother and more compact, in contrast to an uneven surface with cracks and lithium dendrites in $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ saturated electrolyte. Cross section image of the cycled Li foil shows around $30\upmu\mathrm{m}$ active Li ( $60\%$ of initial capacity, Fig. S11) remains after 240 cycles in $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{LNO@MOF}}$ electrolyte, while no active Li remains in $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ saturated electrolyte, which agrees well with the residual lithium from the reassemble Li|SS cell (Fig.  4b). Moreover, the passivation layer $(16\upmu\mathrm{m})$ is much thinner than that $(40\upmu\mathrm{m})$ of the cycled Li in $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ saturated electrolyte. LCO|Li cell with thick Li anode $(400~{\upmu\mathrm{m}})$ and thin LCO cathode $(1.5~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ was also tested (Fig. S12). Cycling stability of the cell with LNO $@$ MOF electrolyte outperforms the cell with blank electrolyte, indicating reduced electrolyte consumption and Li pulverization during cycling due to the stable SEI formed in $\mathrm{LNO}@\mathrm{MOF}$ electrolyte.
The rate capability of LCO|Li cells with $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{LNO}}@{\mathrm{MOF}}$ electrolyte was evaluated. As shown in Fig. 4c, for the cell with blank electrolyte, a rapid capacity decline occurs from $1\mathrm{C}$ and little capacity remains at $2\mathrm{C}$ . In comparison, the cell with $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{LNO@MOF}}$ performs better capacity at high charge rates with the stable specific capacities of 128, 116 and $75~\mathrm{mAh~g^{-1}}$ at 0.5, 1 and $2\mathrm{C}$ , respectively, presenting rapid electrochemical kinetics at high rates for practical LMBs. The LCO|Li full cell in $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{LNO}}@{\mathrm{MOF}}$ electrolyte at higher cutoff voltage $(4.5\mathrm{V})$ was also studied. As shown in Fig. 4d, LCO|Li full cell with blank electrolyte displays a sharp decrease in capacity with only $69\%$ capacity retention at the $100^{\mathrm{th}}$ cycle. In contrast, with LNO $@$ MOF electrolyte, the cycling performance greatly improves, retaining $80\%$ of the capacity after 160 cycles. Voltage profiles shown in Fig. S13 reveal gradually increased polarization possibly related to cathode degradation at high potential, to which the inferior cycling stability could be attributed.
Capacity retentions of practical LMBs with high-voltage cathodes in recent studies are summarized in Fig. 4e and Table S3. Considering that durability of practical LMBs is strongly correlated with available active Li and different N/P ratios in previous studies prevent direct comparison of the cycle life, a normalized cycle number is adopted here (see caption of Fig. 4 for details). The cycling performance of LCO|Li cells with LNO $@$ MOF electrolyte outperforms most of the reported results. In particular, the LCO|Li cell cycling between 3.0 and $4.2\mathrm{V}$ is in the long-cycle-life region (above $80\%$ capacity retention for 200 cycles with a low N/P ratio below 3), where very few works have been achieved. The $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{LNO@MOF}}$ nanocapsules in this work provide a promising solution to extend the cycle life of practical LMBs.
## 4  Conclusions
In summary, we have demonstrated LNO $@$ MOF nanocapsules by absorbing $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ into pores of MOF-808 nanoparticles to stabilize LMA. The nanocapsules provide continuous release of $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ into electrolyte and overcome the solubility limitation of $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ in carbonate-based electrolyte. The dissolved $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ preferentially reduced on LMA, forming robust and highly ionic conductive SEI that guarantees uniform Li deposition. As a result, the cycle life of thin Li anode in $50~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ increases from 112 to $250\mathrm{~h~}$ and a higher average Li cycling efficiency of $98.8\%$ is obtained. Outstanding cycling performance is achieved in LCO|Li full cell with a low N/P ratio of 3.3, showing a high capacity retention of $90\%$ after 240 cycles. This work demonstrates an effective strategy to utilize SEI-forming additives with low solubility for practical LMB.
